╭─ Patch Generation ─────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Okay, let’s address this issue.                                    │
│                                                                    │
│ Reasoning:                                                         │
│                                                                    │
│ The core problem is that Django’s save() method, when called on a  │
│ Derived object after it has been reset to have a None primary key  │
│ (uid), overwrites the Item object instead of updating it           │
│ correctly. This happens because Django’s ORM, when encountering a  │
│ None primary key, attempts to create a new record in the database, │
│ effectively replacing the existing Item object.  The reset()       │
│ method correctly sets uid to None, but the subsequent save() call  │
│ isn't behaving as expected. The fix is to ensure that the Item     │
│ object is saved before the Derived object. This will preserve the  │
│ Item’s state and correctly update the Derived object’s primary     │
│ key.                                                               │
│                                                                    │
